    "slug": "array-contract",
    "comp": "m1.c1",
    "title": "Create arrays and read shape size and dtype",
    "before": "Complete Python Fundamentals. Review sequences, numeric expressions and zero-based indexing. NumPy arrays have explicit shape and dtype contracts that Python lists do not provide.",
    "intro": "A weather team stores three days of measurements from two sensors. Before calculating anything, it needs a shared convention: rows are days, columns are sensors, and each value is a numeric reading. Shape describes this structure; dtype describes how individual values are represented.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "An ndarray has axes and a shared element type",
        "paragraphs": [
          "NumPy ndarray supports homogeneous element storage with shape metadata. For a two-dimensional readings array, shape (3, 2) means axis 0 has three positions and axis 1 has two. ndim counts axes, size counts elements and dtype describes the stored values. A one-dimensional shape (6,) is not interchangeable with a two-dimensional shape (3, 2), even when both contain six elements.",
          "Create arrays from regular nested sequences or use constructors such as zeros, ones and arange. Specify an appropriate dtype when storage range or downstream behavior matters. A Python list can mix arbitrary objects; an ordinary numeric ndarray instead uses one numeric dtype. Inferred types can change when the input includes floats or strings, so inspect the result at data boundaries."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Distinguish representation from meaning",
        "paragraphs": [
          "A rectangular numeric shape does not encode physical units, sensor names or business meaning. Maintain an external axis convention and validate dimensions before combining datasets. Two arrays of equal shape can still describe different entities or units.",
          "The example creates actual NumPy arrays and inspects metadata and payload bytes. nbytes covers the array element buffer; it is not a full process-memory measurement and does not include every object or allocator overhead."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "problem": "Inspect a synthetic day-by-sensor array and its numeric payload.",
    "code": "import numpy as np\na = np.array([[12, 14], [11, 15], [13, 16]], dtype=np.int16)\nprint(a.shape, a.ndim, a.size)\nprint(str(a.dtype), a.nbytes)\nprint(np.zeros((2, 3), dtype=np.int8).tolist())",
    "expect": "(3, 2) 2 6\nint16 12\n[[0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0]]",
    "steps": [
      "Create a rectangular array with an explicit 16-bit integer dtype.",
      "Read axes, total element count and payload size.",
