  {
    "slug": "indexing-memory",
    "comp": "m1.c1",
    "title": "Index arrays and distinguish views from independent selections",
    "before": "Complete Python Fundamentals. Review sequences, numeric expressions and zero-based indexing. NumPy arrays have explicit shape and dtype contracts that Python lists do not provide.",
    "intro": "A quality analyst extracts a portion of a measurement array, adjusts an outlier and unexpectedly changes the original dataset. Correct indexing includes understanding both selected values and whether the selection shares the underlying buffer.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Read indexing syntax from the axis contract",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Use a[row, column] for a scalar in a two-dimensional array, a[:, column] for a column and a[start:stop] for a half-open row slice. Negative indices count from the end. Integer indices remove an axis; a slice such as column:column+1 retains that axis. A boolean mask selects positions where its condition is true.",
          "Basic array slices are views that share data, so mutating a writable slice can affect the original. Advanced indexing with integer arrays or boolean selection returns a copy when retrieving values. Assignment directly through an index, such as a[mask] = value, updates the indexed target; do not confuse that assignment with mutating a previously retrieved selection."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Make ownership explicit at mutation boundaries",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Use copy when an independent working buffer is required, and use shares_memory for a targeted check when teaching or debugging shared storage. Assignment such as b = a creates another reference to the same array rather than a new data buffer. Avoid relying on incidental ownership assumptions after complicated indexing.",
          "The fixture contrasts a basic slice, an integer-array selection and an explicit copy. It changes each result to show actual NumPy behavior. This is a local ownership demonstration, not a general guarantee about an arbitrary third-party array implementation."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "problem": "Observe shared slice mutation and independent advanced indexing.",
    "code": "import numpy as np\na = np.arange(6).reshape(2, 3)\nview = a[:, 1:]\nselected = a[[0, 1], [1, 2]]\nindependent = a.copy()\nview[0, 0] = 99\nselected[0] = -1\nprint(a.tolist())\nprint(selected.tolist())\nprint(independent.tolist())\nprint(np.shares_memory(a, view), np.shares_memory(a, selected))",
    "expect": "[[0, 99, 2], [3, 4, 5]]\n[-1, 5]\n[[0, 1, 2], [3, 4, 5]]\nTrue False",
    "steps": [
