    "title": "Reshape with element counts and explicit layout assumptions",
    "before": "Complete Python Fundamentals. Review sequences, numeric expressions and zero-based indexing. NumPy arrays have explicit shape and dtype contracts that Python lists do not provide.",
    "intro": "A retailer reshapes a flat stream of twelve readings into stores by time slots. The code can produce many valid shapes, but only one agrees with the documented ordering. A successful reshape establishes structural feasibility rather than business correctness.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Conserve elements and declare ordering",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Reshape changes the axis structure while retaining the same number of elements. One dimension may be inferred with -1 when the remaining dimensions determine it unambiguously. Six values can become shape (2, 3) or (3, 2), but not (4, 2). Default C-order reshaping follows the specified logical ordering; it does not infer which axis represents a store.",
          "Transpose permutes axes and therefore changes which index identifies a dimension. Transposing a two-dimensional matrix swaps rows and columns, while a one-dimensional array has no second axis to swap. To create a column vector from shape (n,), use an explicit singleton axis or reshape to (n, 1)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Do not infer copying from the method name",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Reshape returns a view when possible and otherwise may copy. Layout, strides and requested order matter, so do not promise shared memory for every reshape. ravel and flatten also have different copying behavior; use an explicit copy if independence is required and test shares_memory when the relation matters.",
          "The fixture reshapes a contiguous array, infers one dimension and demonstrates the difference between a vector and a column. Its memory relationship is verified for this particular input rather than generalized to every strided array."
        ]
      }
