    "slug": "vector-operations",
    "comp": "m2.c1",
    "title": "Express elementwise transformations without Python item loops",
    "before": "Complete Python Fundamentals. Review sequences, numeric expressions and zero-based indexing. NumPy arrays have explicit shape and dtype contracts that Python lists do not provide.",
    "intro": "A utility company converts a million synthetic meter readings from one unit to another. The formula is the same for every element. NumPy can express this as array arithmetic, but replacing syntax alone does not guarantee correct types, units or resource use.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Use array operations with the same mathematical contract",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Universal functions and array operators apply numeric work across arrays. With compatible shapes, addition and multiplication are elementwise. Multiplying two matrices with * does not perform matrix multiplication; @ expresses the matrix-product contract. Start from the intended formula, check shapes and compare a small reviewed input before measuring throughput.",
          "Boolean expressions also operate elementwise. Parenthesize comparisons and combine masks with & or |; Python and/or do not perform elementwise array logic. Use a mask or np.where for conditional results, while remembering that expressions passed as arguments are normally evaluated before where selects results."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Measure the implementation rather than the notation",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Array operations often move loops into compiled code and reduce Python dispatch overhead. np.vectorize is primarily a convenience wrapper and does not make an arbitrary Python callback a compiled numeric kernel. Chained expressions can allocate large temporary arrays; profile representative data when memory or speed matters.",
          "The fixture compares a small loop result with actual array arithmetic and applies a boolean condition. It verifies the transformation and mask on these values, not a performance claim for every workload or hardware configuration."
        ]
      }
