    "slug": "missing-values",
    "comp": "m3.c1",
    "title": "Handle NaN and nonfinite values with an explicit validity policy",
    "before": "Complete Python Fundamentals. Review sequences, numeric expressions and zero-based indexing. NumPy arrays have explicit shape and dtype contracts that Python lists do not provide.",
    "intro": "A monitoring array has one missing reading. Replacing it with zero makes totals easy to compute but changes the meaning of an average. Missingness and nonfinite values need a declared policy rather than a convenient arithmetic shortcut.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Detect the value class deliberately",
        "paragraphs": [
          "NaN is a floating-point marker used in many numerical missing-data workflows. It is not equal to itself, so use isnan rather than equality with np.nan. isfinite rejects both NaN and positive or negative infinity. An ordinary integer dtype cannot represent NaN directly; use a suitable representation rather than silently inventing a numeric sentinel without documentation.",
          "Ordinary mean propagates NaN. nanmean ignores NaN values but does not mean ignore every invalid observation: infinities and domain-specific invalid codes need separate handling. All-NaN groups require an explicit outcome and can produce warnings and NaN results; detect them when that case matters rather than claiming every group has a valid average."
        ]
