        "heading": "Binary floating point approximates many decimal fractions",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Floating-point formats represent a finite set of values. Operations can introduce rounding and the order of a reduction can affect results. Expressions such as 0.1 + 0.2 need not equal 0.3 bit for bit. Greater precision can reduce some errors but cannot make every decimal fraction exactly representable or remove the need for algorithmic care.",
          "Use isclose or allclose when approximate comparison is appropriate, with chosen absolute and relative tolerances. In NumPy the common comparison uses absolute difference against atol plus rtol times the absolute reference value, so the reference scale matters. A default tolerance may be unsuitable near zero, for strict engineering limits or for exact accounting."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Choose tolerances from requirements",
